{-
   An authored `Copy` subtracts `TrivialCopy` - `doc/spec/ownership.md`'s "an authored `Copy`
   subtracts `TrivialCopy`".

   # resolver: ownership

   The whole assertion is in the goldens rather than in the answer, and that is what this fixture is
   for. `Counter` and `Plain` have identical members, identical sizes and identical arithmetic; the
   only difference is that one of them writes `instance Copy`. So every position below resolves to
   `copyTrivial` for one and to a move or a loan for the other, and `.mir.expect` is where that is
   visible - a run that printed the same number either way would assert nothing at all.

   `Counter` is also the first type in the suite that is **tracked without being linear**: it has no
   teardown, so nothing runs when it dies and there is not a single `drop` line for it, and its uses
   are still moves so that a use after one is the diagnostic in `Reject.AuthoredCopy.yana`. That
   combination is what `isTracked`'s `|| !trivialCopy` term exists for and what nothing reached
   before this rule.
-}

data Counter {id: I32}

-- Nothing here calls this. The instance is read by `classifyTypes` for its *existence* - what the
-- author is saying is that duplicating a `Counter` is not the bytes - and the call that dispatches
-- to it is a class call this stage has no solver for yet.
instance Copy(Counter):
    fn copy(from: Counter) -> Counter = Counter {id: from.id}

-- The same members and no instance, which is the control for every function below.
data Plain {id: I32}

fn takeCounter(->c: Counter) -> I32 = c.id
fn peekCounter(c: Counter) -> I32 = c.id

fn takePlain(->p: Plain) -> I32 = p.id
fn peekPlain(p: Plain) -> I32 = p.id

-- A sink position. `p` survives its own handover because a trivial copy is what filled the
-- parameter; `c` does not, and reading it afterwards is the rejection fixture's first function.
fn plainSurvivesTheSink() -> I32:
    let p = Plain {id: 3}
    return takePlain(p) + p.id

fn counterMoves() -> I32:
    let c = Counter {id: 7}
    return takeCounter(c)

-- A second name. `q` is a second *value* and `d` is a shared loan, so the two names below are the
-- same text describing different things - which is the binding half of the same rule.
fn plainSecondName() -> I32:
    let p = Plain {id: 4}
    let q = p
    return q.id + p.id

fn counterSecondName() -> I32:
    let c = Counter {id: 5}
    let d = c
    return d.id + c.id

-- `let &` on both, which is the position `doc/spec/ownership.md` used to say was never affected and
-- now says is affected on the binding axis. The trivial one is copied at `&` exactly as at the other
-- two spellings, so `m` is its own storage and writing it leaves `n` at 1; the authored one is an
-- exclusive loan, so writing `e` writes `c`. `w` reads the loan out before `c` is named again,
-- because a shared read of `c` while `e` is live is the exclusivity rule and not this one.
fn writtenThrough() -> I32:
    let &n = Plain {id: 1}
    let &m = n
    m.id = m.id + 1

    let &c = Counter {id: 1}
    let &e = c
    e.id = e.id + 1
    let w = e.id

    return n.id * 1000 + m.id * 100 + c.id * 10 + w

-- Handed over and then filled again. There is no teardown to place either side of the write, which
-- is what makes this different from `PartialMove.yana`'s `refilled` rather than a copy of it.
fn reinitialized() -> I32:
    let &c = Counter {id: 2}
    let n = takeCounter(c)
    c = Counter {id: 9}
    return n + peekCounter(c)

-- Borrowed rather than moved, so the value is still here afterwards. A `Counter` in a shared
-- position is a loan for the same reason a `String` is.
fn borrowed() -> I32:
    let c = Counter {id: 6}
    return peekCounter(c) + peekCounter(c)

fn main() -> I32:
    return plainSurvivesTheSink() + counterMoves() + plainSecondName() + counterSecondName() +
           writtenThrough() + reinitialized() + borrowed()
